#Automating EC2 Deployment with User Data:

• Using user_data allows you to automatically bootstrap your instance (e.g., install a web server) the moment the VM is launched, without manual interaction.
1. Setup the Bootstrap Script
First, create a shell script file on your machine that Terraform will execute during the instance creation.

# Create the script file

sudo vi install_httpd.sh

›Contents of install_httpd.sh:

#!/bin/bash
sudo yum install httpd -y
cd /var/www/html
echo "<html><h1>Hii!</h1></html>" > index.html
sudo systemctl start httpd

2. Configure the Terraform Script (tf_script.tf)

• Update your main Terraform configuration to include the user_data argument.

provider "aws" {
  region     = "ap-south-1"
  access_key = "..."
  secret_key = "..."
}

resource "aws_instance" "linux_vm" {
  ami             = "ami-id-xyz"
  instance_type   = "t2.micro"
  key_name        = "Ankush-Key-Pair"
  security_groups = ["default"]
  user_data       = file("install_httpd.sh")

  tags = {
    Name = "Alien-X"
  }
}

3. Execution Lifecycle

• Once the files are created, run your standard workflow to provision the infrastructure:
›terraform init
›terraform fmt
›terraform validate
›terraform plan
›terraform apply --auto-approve

#Outcome:
A server named "Alien-X" is created. You can access the static website by visiting the Public IP of the instance in your browser.

*Note: If the page doesn't load, ensure your Security Group inbound rules allow HTTP/HTTPS traffic.
